  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Nidamanuri Rama Rao vs The State Of Ap on 18 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5160 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners/accused seek to quash the proceedings against them in C.C.No.600 of 2018 on the file of the Additional Judicial First Class Magistrate, Addanki, where cognizance was taken against them for the offences under Sections 353, 273, 188 r/w 34 of IPC and Sec. 58, 59 and 63 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (for short 'FSS Act, 2006). 2. The allegations against the petitioner briefly are that on 14.12.2017, when the complainant along with his staff visited kirana shop and beedi bunk at Puttavaripalem cross road, they found two persons trying to hide plastic bags under the tables on enquiry, they revealed that they are selling the tobacco products illegally, the Police seized the same under the cover of mediator's report. After the investigation, police filed charge sheet for the offences stated supra. 3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore, the Police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. He relied upon the order of this Court in Crl.P.Nos.3731 of 2018 and batch, dated 27.08.2018.",
    "arguments": "So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
